WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
How do you work out where a neuron came from, and what it needs for the trip?

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.040
There are three ways to ask.

3
00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:11.463
Follow the cells out.

4
00:00:12.263 --> 00:00:13.263
Remove the source.

5
00:00:13.963 --> 00:00:16.523
Or swap tissue, and see which one the defect follows.

6
00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:24.357
First, follow. In 1996, dye placed in the lateral eminence of cultured rat embryos

7
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:26.457
marked cells that reached the cortex.

8
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.640
But dye fades, and it does not say what a cell becomes.

9
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.593
A genetic label fixes that.

10
00:00:34.613 --> 00:00:40.053
Cells that switch on Nkx2.1, mostly in the medial eminence, turn on a reporter.

11
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:45.183
Every descendant keeps the reporter, even after the gene turns off.

12
00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:50.793
In mature mouse cortex, many marked cells are inhibitory interneurons.

13
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:58.873
Second, remove. In 1997, Stewart Anderson, John Rubenstein and colleagues cut mouse

14
00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:00.873
cortex away from the ventral forebrain.

15
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:05.927
The isolated cortex ended up with fewer cells containing GABA.

16
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.280
They also used a knockout, a mouse with a gene disrupted in every cell.

17
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.990
They disrupted both Dlx1 and Dlx2.

18
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.410
These genes encode transcription factors made in the subpallium.

19
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.180
In the mutant cortex, which neurons go missing?

20
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:32.033
The inhibitory ones.

21
00:01:32.953 --> 00:01:37.813
No migration from the ventral forebrain into the neocortex was detected, and few

22
00:01:37.813 --> 00:01:38.933
cells contained GABA.

23
00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:42.043
The pyramidal neurons were still made.

24
00:01:42.923 --> 00:01:44.823
Their progenitors do not need these genes.

25
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:51.767
But a knockout lacks the genes in every cell.

26
00:01:52.607 --> 00:01:54.707
Is the fault in the travellers, or in the road?

27
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.880
In 2001, the same group answered with grafts.

28
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:04.923
They placed labelled pieces of eminence into forebrain slices, crossing normal and

29
00:02:04.923 --> 00:02:06.343
mutant tissue both ways.

30
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:11.517
A normal piece in a mutant slice, or a mutant piece in a normal slice.

31
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:13.377
Which fails to migrate?

32
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:23.487
Normal cells migrated into the cortex of mutant slices.

33
00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:28.537
Mutant cells failed to migrate into the cortex of normal slices.

34
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:31.387
The defect travelled with the cells.

35
00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:40.217
So whatever these genes do for the trip, they do it inside the travelling cells.

36
00:02:42.017 --> 00:02:45.177
The swap shows where the need lies, not how the genes act.

37
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.770
For Dlx, the swap gives a clear answer.

38
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.090
The defect travels with the cell.
